  "review_scope": "All sixteen recursive author inputs receive a complete original read and two complete revised mathematical/editorial reads, including every proof; the second follows the first build and complete visual review. All 44 formal statements, 34 proofs and 103 labels remain unchanged after removing index commands, local spacing controls and whitespace. The chapter retains fifteen sections and seven subsections, with no figures. Chapter-local display pagination and targeted opening/statement guards are checked in the final output. Other author inputs and the bibliography are unchanged.",
  "mathematical_changes": "Chapter 27 now connects positivity of a closed class, mass of a specified divisorial trace and metric completeness. Its opening explains why these are distinct questions. The density examples are reconciled with the fixed regular locus in the interior-smoothness theorem, and the curve-product transition compares its single-logarithm threshold with the radial iterated-logarithm thresholds. The divisorial threshold opening separates annihilation of the class by a weak limit from attainment under uniform integrability.",
  "mathematical_boundaries": "The restricted-class supremum remains different from the mass of a specified trace and from the numerical restricted volume outside the nef regime. Positive residual mass gives only a one-sided singularity comparison unless its specified model has a smooth positive residual representative. Divisorial annihilation uses the weak closure of smooth Gauduchon tests; uniform integrability along the chosen sequence supplies attainment. The elliptic example retains the distinction between closed cones, fixed-density volume and raw mass. Interior smoothness requires smooth positive density on the entire regular locus of the fixed strict analytic barrier. The compact Pogorelov gluing has nonsmooth density along an open part of its divisor; an analytic polar set containing that part also contains the inner continuation. It does not give a local version of the global regularity theorem. The radial boundedness and length integrals retain their distinct thresholds, including n < d <= 2n for bounded continuous complete ends. Exceptional density order is necessary, not sufficient, for a smooth residual. The exponential examples retain the distinction between integrable logarithmically weakened poles and a prescribed nonintegrable pole with smooth positive coefficient or flow-compatible data.",
  "dependencies": "Bounded internal dependency reads cover the Chapter 13 fixed-barrier setup, interior-smoothness statement and concluding proof, and global-Lp corollary; the Chapter 1 proper-gap lemma and proof; the Chapter 22 bounded-closed-potential nef lemma and proof; and the relevant closed-locus, regularity and completeness history passages. The Chapter 26 ending and Chapter 28 opening are checked. Classical source checks recorded in checkpoints133 and160, reused in checkpoint173, retain their prior scopes. The full source-TeX reconstruction recorded in checkpoint211 is reused; this checkpoint freshly reads the three Q2.10 inputs within Chapter 27, not all six integrated inputs in Chapters 1, 13 and 27. No fresh external source-proof audit, independent verifier run, Popovici input or novelty claim is made.",
  "all_preexisting_formal_statements_and_proofs_unchanged_after_index_spacing_and_whitespace_normalization": true,
